## Safe prerequisites
- Preserve the original artifact hash, package/version and decompilation limitations.
- Confirm the exact researcher-owned app, current Android build and dedicated device serial.
- Use synthetic accounts, resources and canaries only; no unrelated apps or destinations.
- Start with an unrooted ordinary attacker context. ADB shell, root and authorization-changing hooks do not establish ordinary-app access.

## Controlled validation
1. Resolve the actual provider authority, URI matcher routes, exported state, read/write/path permissions and URI grant policy. These are not inferred from the app package or class label.
2. Trace the actual query/openFile implementation, parameter binding and canonical path containment. A method name alone does not prove injection or traversal.
3. For a file candidate, provision a harmless synthetic marker outside the intended exposed directory in the research fixture. Test read-only access through an ordinary-app harness, then compare allowed-path and blocked-path controls.
4. For a query candidate, use a synthetic database and authorized read-only requests with known result counts. Confirm that attacker-controlled syntax changes the query rather than merely returning an error or expected rows.
5. Do not read real credentials, enumerate unrelated storage, insert/delete records or assume ADB shell provider access reproduces ordinary-app access.

## Evidence and conclusion
Record the exact entrypoint, attacker-controlled input, security boundary, sensitive sink, observed impact and commands/test-app source actually used. Attach redacted evidence plus successful authorized and rejected unauthorized controls. Distinguish unsupported observations, negative results and reproduced impact. No outcome or attacker capability is asserted by this plan.

